bench7 - how to run it
======================

Machines (all in one private network):
  app host      2 vCPU, runs the framework (1-VM setup: also Postgres)
  db host       2-VM setup only: Postgres alone
  generator 1   load generator + coordinator (has this repo)
  generator 2.. extra load generators (plain Ubuntu, no repo needed)

How many generators (8 vCPU each): 1-VM needs 2 (it tops out near 17k RPS). 2-VM needs 4: with 2
the run stops on "generator CPU >= 90%" around 24k, with 4 it reaches ~30k.

Placeholders used below:
  <repo-url>     this repository
  <app-ip>       private IP of the app host
  <db-ip>        private IP of the DB host (2-VM setup)
  <user>@<gen-N-ip>  login of extra generator N

Every script takes --help and is safe to run again (finished steps are skipped).
Secrets live in bench7.env (created on first use, never committed, never print it).


0. Get the code  (app host, DB host, generator 1)
-------------------------------------------------
  git clone <repo-url> ~/bench7 && cd ~/bench7        # later: git pull


1. Tune Linux  (every machine)
------------------------------
  ./tune_linux.sh                 # then log out and in again (open-file limit)


2. Postgres  (the DB machine: app host for 1-VM, DB host for 2-VM)
-------------------------------------------------------------------
  ./setup_postgres.sh             # /data on the local NVMe (40 GB) + Postgres 18, started
  ./setup_postgres.sh --data-gb 80    # DB-only host with room for the full 29 GB seed

then ONE of:

  2.1  1-VM (app on this same machine)
  ./tune_postgres1.sh             # optional: --set name=value ... (e.g. --set commit_delay=0)

  2.2  2-VM (Postgres alone here)
  ./tune_postgres2.sh             # opens 5432 to 10.0.0.0/8 (--allow CIDR), starts the DB sampler :41902
                                  # optional: --set name=value ...

After a VM stop/start the local NVMe can come back blank: run ./setup_postgres.sh again,
it re-creates the cluster and applies the last tune_postgres command; then seed again.


3. Seed the database  (same machine as step 2)
----------------------------------------------
  ./seed_db.sh --size-gb 3        # ~3 GB, about 2 min on 2 vCPU
  ./seed_db.sh                    # full data set, ~29 GB, about 33 min
  ./seed_db.sh --reseed --size-gb 3   # drop and load again
Runs detached (seed.log); Ctrl-C only stops the progress view, ./seed_db.sh follows it again.
Compare results only between runs on the same seed size.
Back to the seed after a test, no reload (seconds):  ./seed_db.sh   (on an already seeded DB)
  deletes only the rows the test wrote, fixes the counters it bumped, vacuums, and checks every
  table has exactly the seed's row count. Refuses while an app is connected: kill it first
  (./kill_<framework>.sh) or add --force. ./run_<framework>.sh does the same delete on start.


4. Toolchains + builds  (app host)
----------------------------------
  ./setup_toolchain.sh axum springboot      # or no argument = all 8 frameworks
Frameworks: axum springboot aspnet gin fastapi node fastify_bun bun
(./run_<framework>.sh also installs/builds on first use; this step just does it ahead of time.)


5. Start a framework  (app host)
--------------------------------
  1-VM:  ./run_axum.sh
  2-VM:  ./run_axum.sh --db-host <db-ip>     # asks once for the DB password:
                                              # on the DB host: grep PG_PASSWORD bench7.env
Options: --build (rebuild)  --no-clean (keep the last test's rows)  --profile 1vm|2vm
         --set KEY=VALUE (app env, wins over the profile, e.g. --set BATCH_WINDOW_MS=200)
Profiles (picked from where the DB is):
  1vm  DB_POOL_TOTAL=12  BATCH_LANES=1  BATCH_MAX_ROWS=200   BATCH_WINDOW_MS=20
  2vm  DB_POOL_TOTAL=24  BATCH_LANES=4  BATCH_MAX_ROWS=1000  BATCH_WINDOW_MS=20
It prints the exact run_k6 command for step 7.

Live view (app host, 2nd terminal):
  ./watch_server.sh               # dashboard
  ./watch_server.sh --log         # one line per second
  ./watch_server.sh --port 41902  # on the DB host (2-VM)


6. Connect the load generators  (generator 1, once)
---------------------------------------------------
An extra generator is a plain Ubuntu 24.04 VM (8 vCPU) in the same private network; it needs no
repo and no public IP. Generator 1 must be able to ssh to it, either:
  a) log in to generator 1 with "ssh -A" (your laptop key is forwarded for the first login), or
  b) put generator 1's key on it: on generator 1 run ./connect_agent.sh once (creates
     ~/.ssh/bench7_k6), then add ~/.ssh/bench7_k6.pub to the generator's ~/.ssh/authorized_keys
     (or pass it as the VM's ssh key when you create the VM).
Then, on generator 1:
  ./connect_agent.sh                                              # k6 on generator 1 only
  ./connect_agent.sh <user>@<gen-2-ip>                            # 2 generators (1-VM)
  ./connect_agent.sh <user>@<gen-2-ip> <user>@<gen-3-ip> <user>@<gen-4-ip>   # 4 generators (2-VM)
It installs k6 + network tuning on each, saves the list in k6-agents.env and prints "connected: ...".
run_k6.sh then starts k6 on all of them (report header: "generators: local, gen-2, ...").
Running it again with a new list replaces the old list; --agents '' on run_k6.sh runs generator 1 alone.


7. Run the load test  (generator 1)
-----------------------------------
  1-VM:
  ./run_k6.sh --ip <app-ip> --rps 1000,5000,21000,50000 --times 10,30,120,285 --warmup 30
  2-VM:
  ./run_k6.sh --ip <app-ip> --db-ip <db-ip> --rps 1000,5000,21000,50000 --times 10,30,120,285 --warmup 30

  --payload-kb 5        write size (default 5 KB; --payload-kb 2 = the older ~2 KB runs)
  --design normal       Group 2 routes (one INSERT per request, no cache) instead of batching
  --name LABEL          results dir label
  --p95 700 --p99 1000 --err 1 --drop 1 --window 5 | --no-stop      stop rules
The command is the same for every framework (the app always listens on <app-ip>:8080).
Report: results/<name>-<time>/report.md  (max RPS within SLO, stop reason, app + DB host metrics)


8. Next framework  (app host)
-----------------------------
  ./kill_axum.sh                  # saves app log + host metrics to results/host/
  ./run_springboot.sh             # then step 7 again

All frameworks, one after the other (app host terminal / generator 1 terminal):
  app host                                  generator 1
  ./run_axum.sh        [--db-host <db-ip>]  ./run_k6.sh ... --name axum
  ./kill_axum.sh
  ./run_springboot.sh  [--db-host <db-ip>]  ./run_k6.sh ... --name springboot
  ./kill_springboot.sh
  ./run_aspnet.sh      [--db-host <db-ip>]  ./run_k6.sh ... --name aspnet
  ./kill_aspnet.sh
  ./run_gin.sh         [--db-host <db-ip>]  ./run_k6.sh ... --name gin
  ./kill_gin.sh
  ./run_fastapi.sh     [--db-host <db-ip>]  ./run_k6.sh ... --name fastapi
  ./kill_fastapi.sh
  ./run_node.sh        [--db-host <db-ip>]  ./run_k6.sh ... --name node
  ./kill_node.sh
  ./run_fastify_bun.sh [--db-host <db-ip>]  ./run_k6.sh ... --name fastify_bun
  ./kill_fastify_bun.sh
  ./run_bun.sh         [--db-host <db-ip>]  ./run_k6.sh ... --name bun
  ./kill_bun.sh
("..." = the step 7 arguments. Each run_ cleans the previous test's rows, so runs are comparable.)


Undo everything on a machine
----------------------------
  sudo ./reset.sh                 # -y: no question, --all: also delete the checkout
Extra generators have no repo: reset them from generator 1 BEFORE resetting generator 1 (the reset
removes the bench7_k6 key that generator 1 logs in with):
  ssh -i ~/.ssh/bench7_k6 <user>@<gen-N-ip> 'sudo bash -s -- -y' < reset.sh
